X-Reposting-Policy: Redistribute freely

To add to Derek's points about the inadequacy of artificial neural networks:
most neural net work assumes that the output of a neuron is a real
number that represents "degree of excitation".  This is said to be
analogous to the rate of pulsing that an actual neuron would produce.
The thing is, there are recent observations that hint that not just
frequency, but phase may be important in real neurons.  The relative
phase between the pulses of different subsystems of neurons has been
observed to correlate with certain forms of information.

Also, of course, the various subsystems of the brain can be separated
by neurotransmitter type, another distinction which is not even considered
in most artificial neural network research.

A few days ago there was mention of the work showing that "dying" neural
networks reverted to earlier-formed behaviors, analogous to the "life
passing before one's eyes" phenomenon.  But the NN results were produced
by randomly severing connections between neurons.  The degraded net
showed these earlier-learned patterns.  People who survive to report
near-death experiences have not had their neural links severed.  So I
don't see that there is any connection between these two phenomena.

Hal Finney
