Date: Sun, 9 May 93 00:30:18 CDT
From: ddfr@midway.uchicago.edu
Subject: A Meme to Squash--BUT HOW?

Jay writes:

>The reason some uncomplicated people want WorldGovernment(tm) is because
>they think that One Big Government *MIGHT* not devote 25-50% of the worlds
>resources on armorments. 

It is not clear whether the number he offers is intended as rhetorical
exaggeration, what he thinks other people believe, or what. It is about an
order of magnitude too high. For 1985, according to the figures in the Stat
Abstracts, worldwide military expenditures were 6.1% of world GNP. I would
expect the number to be significantly lower now. 

Jay goes on to write:

>They think that this might be worth a lot of WelfareCheats(tm) and
>MindlessBureaucracy(tm) and might actually end up producing
>MorePieForEverybody(tm). Think of how much money you could literally BURN
>and still have more resources devoted to churing out consumer goods.

Given how small a fraction of world GNP is devoted to armaments, it would
not take much governmental inefficiency to make up for the savings, even if
we assume that the world government spent almost nothing on armaments.

Of course, this still leaves another and stronger argument--that even if it
did not save much money, world peace would save a lot of lives. One obvious
counterargument is that civil wars can still exist within one country, and
are often very bloody--on a per capita basis, the American Civil War was, I
am pretty sure, the worst war the U.S. was ever in.

In the post-WW II period, I can think of at least four internal conflicts
that were worse than all save the largest international wars. The Nigerian
Civil War, Cambodia, Burundi, Uganda, possibly Indonesia--and those are
only the big ones I can think of. The only international wars on that scale
that occur to me are Korea and Vietnam, which were both partly civil wars,
and Iraq-Iran, which I think was the biggest post WWII war in terms of
casualties.

David Friedman
University of Chicago Law School


