Date: Sun, 9 May 93 10:40:44 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: INFO: Channel capacity and Reversible logic

>Can anyone point me to an information theoretic derivation of Shannon's
>channel capacity formula?  I'm interested because I read that the speed
>limitations on reversible nanotech is a consequence of channel capacity.
>
>If anyone can help me out I would be grateful.
>
>Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

I haven't seen this point explicitly dealt with. Any references?

"Elements of Information Theory," by Thomas Cover and Joy A. Thomas, 1991,
remains my favorite. Very theoretical, but with lots of stuff on Occam's
razor, Kolmogorov-Chaitin complexity, etc. Nothing directly on "reversible
computation," that I can see.

For that, try the latest "Proceedings of the Workshop on Physics and
Computation (PhysComp '92)," IEEE Computer Society Press, 1993. No direct
comments on how the speed of computation depends on the Shannon channel
capacity, that I could find.

This sounds plausible...one would not expect a reversible computer to
somehow violate the channel capacity theorem (if so, one could ignore the
computer part and just use it to send bits at beyond the channel
capacity!).

Copying and uncopying the "garbage bits" (which of course are generated
prolifically in reversible computation schemes) is one are where the
channel capacity certainly enters. What may be involved in the gates
themselves is probably another area.

Let us know what you find out.


