Subject: Why does complicated accounting fail on this List?

Timothy C. May writes:

>This is a question I've been wondering about: Why do schemes involving
>complicated accounting and pricing work in the "real world" but fail to
>catch on on this list?

As far as it relates to me and my own suggestion in "Meta: Opportunity
for Spontaneous Order (Long)" that I posted on the list a while ago:
I've been somewhat lazy.

(I have been meaning to write some test code.  I'll up the priority of
that project.)

>1. Too complicated. Unlike, say, buying groceries or paying electric bills,
>these schemes are complicated (I won't get into any in particular) and have
>confusing rules, costs, benefits, etc.

This will get into particulars, but...

You've said this sort of thing a number of times, and I really don't
understand it at all.

Okay, the HEx system does seem almost incomprehensible to me,
(sorry, Brian), but to drag in at least one particular system,
I don't see the complications in a scheme where:

  1.  If you do nothing at all, you will see no difference.

  2.  If you switch subscriptions to the alternate list,
      the only difference is that:

      A.  All posts suddenly list a current net worth of the poster.

      Implying that:

      B.  If you suddenly start posting worthless posts, your listed
          net worth goes down.
      C.  If you keep posting wonderful things, your listed net worth
          goes up.

  3.  Thus, if you work real hard on one post, and believe a lot of
      people will be interested, you can bet a lot on that one post,
      in the belief that you'll get a good return on an excellent post.

      If you want to make a "Me, too" post, it would probably not be
      wise to bet a lot on it.

  3.  If you want to have a voice in the listed net worths of the other
      posters, simply vote on each post, and mail in your tally.

      (Although in practice, this should simply mean typing R85 <return>
      [Ranking, 85 out of a hundred] in your mailreader, or something
      similar.)

      Those who actually care to have a voice in the list's net worth
      listings can set up their mailreaders to mail back their proxies.

      Someone who thinks it is too much trouble to vote can simply
      not bother and so have no voice.

  4.  A central banking algorithm was extensively discussed in my
      previous post, and in my mind seems necessary in order to have
      the advantages of a centralized rating system.  (That is,
      a rating seemingly given by the Exi list itself.)

      However, that doesn't mean that centralized and decentralized
      versions of the betting markets can't exist simultaneously.
      Whereas the centralized version would have the advantage of
      tending to keep the list together (fewer flying yogi posts),
      the decentralized version should prove extremely useful
      to the individual reader, helping them sort postings, as
      well as forming webs of reputation, as I mentioned in my
      previous post.

      And of course, the decentralized version is something that could
      and should be implemented on the net itself, if it can be fine-tuned
      someplace such as here.

>The challenge will be for us to design *very simple* systems with very simple
>*things* being bought and sold. (Anything that involves a series of
>calculations to figure out is not going to go over big here, in my
>opinion).

But it is very simple.

You simply bet some amount on each post (optional),
rate each post (optional), view the results (optional),
and learn from the resultant information (optional).

If you really like it, you can set up some scripts
on your machine to help (optional).

In no event need a person ever do actually do any math
other than pulling numbers out of thin air, and even
that is, surprise, optional.

>2. The mail problem. People are using a variety of mail programs and so
>have different communication constraints...witness the difficulty in
>getting encryption made more universal or in getting beyond simple ASCII
>for these messages. Any scheme that has payments attached to posts (even if
>in only in "token" money)  will have to work robustly for a dozen or so
>types of mail systems.

True, but no web of reputation can come about without some work on
the part of the users.  It's similar to trying to get a PGP web of
trust in place among people who don't want to bother with creating
keys.  There's no way around that problem.

If a person wants to use their software without upgrading, without
editing any headers or anything, with only typing in a "subject:"
line and a "to:" line, and with a body of the message that is only
the message itself, then there's nothing I can suggest except working
around that person.  (Which my proposal does.)

But even that worst case scenario doesn't have much an effect on
everyone else.  That person's posts are still rated, and even though
he can't vote or change his bets, or possibly even see his current net
worth, that net worth will still go up and down with the quality of
his posts, and he will still be entered into a web of reputation
with other people, judged by those who are interested in participating.

I see no problem here.  The ones who use the system benefit by it, and
those who don't use it still benefit.  (Unless they continually write
awful posts.)

>(True, it can be done inside the ASCII text files,
>manually in most cases, but this places the burden of accounting and
>calculation on the user. Few people would use Visa cards if they had to
>perform the necessary calculations, phone connections, etc.)

But people do use visa cards, and they do perform the "necessary" (for
them) calculations and connections.

If I want to order something by mail, I actually have to call
a merchant and then tell him my Visa card number.  He then actually
tells me what it will cost to the cent, after including his shipping
costs, etc., and I actually have to pay that out of a checking account,
or set my account up to pay it automatically.

I don't have to figure the compounded interest myself; the
bank does that for me.  But I can still do it by hand if I want.

The Visa analogy is fairly close: In both cases I need not do
any math other than pulling relevant numbers out of the air;
if I wanted to, I could do a great deal of math myself
by budgeting my purchases in relation to some long home-
accounting formulas.

In any event, the amount of work, and the benefits gotten,
are all up to each individual.

